                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "preterites"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3e383443-9902-3d39-a5a5-4914b7575421",
                        "entry_id": "a2026e05-d63f-3c10-a770-159f2e95e756",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A grammatical tense or verb form serving to denote events that took place or were completed in the past."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c83f76a1-6282-4afb-bae0-0618663108f0",
                                "sense_id": "3e383443-9902-3d39-a5a5-4914b7575421",
                                "text": "When simple verbs redouble the preterite, the compounds drop the first syllable, as: Pello, pĕpŭli, to drive away, to beat back; Repello, rĕpŭli, and not rĕpĕpŭli, to drive back, to repel."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "93ffdd77-03c1-4f89-bdc4-f3ce6e1ab408",
                                "sense_id": "3e383443-9902-3d39-a5a5-4914b7575421",
                                "text": "Nevertheless, a small amount of variation still exists in one area of standard English verbal morphology: the preterite and past participle forms of certain irregular verbs."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e2bfe8b1-0569-4f90-9828-3d7d6675fe2a",
